} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7585a86b-d9f0-55bc-8e6f-efb92e7d43c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398981} :answer-field/id #uuid "29184956-f54b-5670-ad89-7254d448c06c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9af406e1-4ead-558e-9219-f0bb53a377fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-74316" :question/problem "Which of the following is a lower triangular matrix?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is a matrix for which all entries *above* the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nHence, it is a lower triangular matrix. \n\nBy contrast, matrices $A$, $C$, $D$, and $E$ each have at least one non-zero entry above the diagonal:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, they are not lower triangular matrices."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Triangular Matrix"}]}
